{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790976148,"nonce":"jRGL4ATfzte263C970nQSLpNInA=","title":"Future resolved through hlp_ symbols found by dlsym never wakes the awaiting fiber","message":"A native library that finds `hlp_future_create` / `hlp_future_resolve` with `dlsym` on the process gets the copies exported by the `ash` executable. Haxe's `ash.Future.await()` runs in the copy in `target/release/libhl.dylib` that Ash loads as the HDLL-compatible std (\"[ash] Loading HDLL-compatible ash_std at .../libhl.dylib\"). The resolve then wakes the executable's scheduler, and the fiber parked in libhl's `fiber::park` (sitting in `Condvar::wait_timeout`) never returns. It works only when the future is already settled before `await` parks.\n\nThe same future works when the library links `hlp_future_*` against libhl directly.\n\nBoth images export the full set:\n- `nm -gU target/release/ash | grep hlp_future` lists await/create/reject/resolve/state, and `hlp_alloc_dynamic` too;\n- `nm -gU target/release/libhl.dylib | grep hlp_future` lists the same.\n\n**Who hits it.** hlwgpu (and so anything on xgpu): `hl_xidl::runtime_symbol` resolves its Ash entry points with `libloading::Library::this()`, which is dlopen(NULL) and searches the main executable first. Every async GPU call (`requestAdapter`, `requestDevice`, `mapBuffer`) goes through this. On ashui's offscreen render, `requestAdapter().await()` hangs 10/10 under `--mode jit`, about 1/10 in hybrid, and never in interp; the difference between modes is only timing.\n\n**Also a GC hazard.** `hlp_alloc_dynamic` and `hlp_type_i32` are found the same way, so hlwgpu boxes future results against the executable's GC and type singletons, not the ones the program runs on.\n\n**Reproducer (no hlwgpu needed):** ashui `tests/fixtures/ash/future_foreign_thread/run.sh [--mode interp|jit]`.\n- `futrepro.c` creates a future and resolves it from a plain pthread with a boxed Int, either linked against libhl (`linked`) or through `dlsym(dlopen(NULL))` (`lookup`).\n- It prints where dlsym finds `hlp_future_resolve` (the `ash` executable).\n- Expected: every row `ok`. Actual: every `lookup` row hangs, in all three modes:\n\n```\nlinked  delay      0us: ok\nlinked  delay   1000us: ok\nlinked  delay  50000us: ok\nlookup  delay      0us: HANG after: dlsym finds hlp_future_resolve in .../ash/target/release/ash first resolved\nlookup  delay   1000us: HANG after: dlsym finds hlp_future_resolve in .../ash/target/release/ash\nlookup  delay  50000us: HANG after: dlsym finds hlp_future_resolve in .../ash/target/release/ash\n```\n\n**Possible fixes**, for whoever owns them:\n- one ash_std per process: the executable stops exporting `hlp_*`, or forwards them to the libhl it loaded;\n- or a documented lookup rule for hdlls (prefer the loaded libhl image), applied in hl_xidl's `runtime_symbol`.\n\nFound at ash 4f371a3, hlwgpu c591807, macOS arm64.","files":null}]}